    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n2\n1 2\n5\n1 2\n2 3\n3 4\n4 5",
        "output": "BR\nRRRBB",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: With the given coloring, cR=cB=1c_R = c_B = 1cR​=cB​=1. Now,\n\nIn node 111's subtree, the number of nodes colored red (111) is equal to the number of nodes colored blue (111). 111 is colored blue, so we add 111 to sBs_BsB​.\nIn node 222's subtree, the number of nodes colored red (111) is greater than the number of nodes colored blue (000), so we add 111 to sRs_RsR​.\n\nSo, ∣cR−cB∣+∣sR−sB∣=∣1−1∣+∣1−1∣=0|c_R - c_B| + |s_R - s_B| = |1-1| + |1-1| = 0∣cR​−cB​∣+∣sR​−sB​∣=∣1−1∣+∣1−1∣=0, which is clearly the minimum possible value.\nTest case 222: For the given coloring, cR=3c_R = 3cR​=3 and cB=2c_B = 2cB​=2. Now,\n\nFor node 111's and 222's subtree: red wins, so sR=2s_R = 2sR​=2.\nFor node 333's, 444's and 555's subtree: blue wins, so sB=3s_B = 3sB​=3.\n\nThis gives us ∣3−2∣+∣2−3∣=1+1=2|3-2| + |2-3| = 1+1 = 2∣3−2∣+∣2−3∣=1+1=2.\nIt can be shown that attaining a lower value is not possible no matter how we color the vertices."
      }
    ]
